1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,927
A Volcano Eruption.
火山喷发。

2
00:00:03,927 --> 00:00:55,920
As the best candidate, I was appointed to collect and evaluate information for a database about diverse natural disasters, such as hurricanes, typhoons or thunderstorms. Sometimes I did this by giving out questionnaires to those who went through them. But sometimes I had to go through actual disasters myself.
作为最合适的人选，我被指派收集并评估信息，用于一个涵盖各种自然灾害的数据库，例如飓风，台风或雷暴。有时，我通过发放调查问卷来收集信息，对象是经历过这些灾害的人。但有时，我也不得不亲身经历真正的灾难。

3
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:42,680
The Musaki Volcano was one of them. At that time, I lived in a bungalow alongside the volcano with a novelist, who came for the appreciation of its eruption. One day, when I was drawing diagrams on the balcony and he was bathing inside, the volcano erupted unexpectedly.
穆萨基火山事件就是其中一次。当时，我和一位小说家住在火山旁的一栋平房里，他是为了观赏火山喷发而来的。一天，我在阳台画图，他在屋里洗澡，火山突然喷发了。

4
00:01:42,680 --> 00:02:16,440
The ash and lava fountains shooting high in the sky varied from 50 to 100 meters in height. It was absolutely fantastic! I even noticed a rainbow appear in the fog caused by a heat wave.
喷向高空的火山灰和熔岩喷泉，高度在50到100米之间不等。那景象真是令人惊叹！我甚至看见雾中出现了一道彩虹，那片雾是热浪形成的。

5
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:51,367
But, glancing through our surroundings, I foresaw the potential danger and found no guarantee of our safety. The uncomfortable gas almost made us unconscious. Sweat began to drip as we grew anxious.
但扫视周围之后，我预感到潜在的危险，觉得我们的安全没有保障。令人不适的气体几乎让我们失去知觉。随着焦虑加剧，汗水开始滴落。

6
00:02:51,367 --> 00:03:27,460
Managing to stop panicking and trembling, we quickly put on our protective suits, helmets and boots and eventually made our way to a safe place. How lucky we were even though my precious equipment and documents were all burnt to the ground!
我们努力让自己不再惊慌、发抖，迅速穿上防护服，戴好头盔、穿好靴子，最终来到安全的地方。我们是多么幸运，尽管我珍贵的设备和文件全被烧毁了！
